{"built_at":"2026-09-15T00:01:33.563137+02:00","git":"9c276a5","version":{"version":"v0.10","date":"2026-09-14T14:30:56+02:00","commit":"9a83940","per_series":{"power_price_SE1":"v0.10","power_price_SE2":"v0.10","power_price_SE3":"v0.10","power_price_SE4":"v0.10","fcr_n_a1":"v0.10","fcr_n_a2":"v0.10","fcr_d_up_a1":"v0.10","fcr_d_up_a2":"v0.10","fcr_d_down_a1":"v0.10","fcr_d_down_a2":"v0.10","mfrr_up":"v0.10","mfrr_down":"v0.10","mfrr_cm_up":"v0.10","mfrr_cm_down":"v0.10"},"spot_only_note":"v0.9 changed how the spot series are fitted; the reserve series carry their v0.8 configuration."},"window":"C","window_def":{"train_end":"2026-02-01","test_end":"2026-09-01"},"windows":{"A":{"train_end":"2025-08-01","test_end":"2026-02-01"},"B":{"train_end":"2025-02-01","test_end":"2025-08-01"},"C":{"train_end":"2026-02-01","test_end":"2026-09-01"}},"mode":"live","proof":{"date":"2026-09-14","issue":"spot","sha256":"772df91878bf1dfc9329b26bde913ac95682f9a66a569f6688e89efe90aa75b0","tsa_time":"Sep 14 09:01:04 2026 GMT","tsa":"https://freetsa.org/tsr","commit":"e49222b","released":false,"released_after":"2026-09-16","repo":"https://github.com/JacobsCodeCom/ancillary-markets"},"contact":{"email":"contact@jacobscode.com","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jacob-ekedahl","repo":"https://github.com/JacobsCodeCom/ancillary-markets"},"not_yet":["Battery allocation across markets: as a view for splitting a battery between markets, the forecasts do not yet beat a 7-day mean. Not offered.","Load shifting against a 7-day mean price profile: clearly positive in SE1 and SE2 at every flexibility level; in SE4 positive, but the 95% range clears zero only for a contiguous four-hour block; in SE3 not positive. Against yesterday's prices it is positive in all four areas.","The record holds each evaluation window's model fixed for up to seven months, while the live service is kept current, so these numbers understate it. Scored the way the live service runs, on nine rolling two-month periods, SE4 beats the 7-day mean at every flexibility level and SE3 for an eight-hour day.","Measured against a professional day-ahead price forecast rather than a rule of thumb, the forecasts are ahead in SE1, SE2 and SE4 (SE4 for an eight-hour day), and level in SE3 - so in SE3 nothing is claimed against a buyer who already has a price forecast.","FCR-D down: no spike alert (no better than persistence) and the hold call does not beat always selling in auction 2.","Auction 1 prices: the weakest error reduction of the FCR series (20-25%); the hold call rests mostly on the auction-2 view.","aFRR capacity prices are not forecast. Forecasts are hourly; day-ahead trades in 15-minute periods since 1 Oct 2025 and we forecast the hourly average."],"properties":[{"heading":"Only the shape of the day matters for a store","text":"A store that must deliver the same heat each day buys the same total energy whatever the prices are. So the level of a price forecast cannot change the schedule: adding 100 SEK/MWh to every hour, or scaling the whole day, leaves the chosen hours identical. What changes the answer is the order and the relative size of the hours - which is what this model is built to get right, and what it is measured on."},{"heading":"A fuller tank is worth more than a lower average error","text":"The schedule is decided once, before the day starts, and the tank can only hold so much. Being close on average matters less than being right about which hours to fill in - an error that moves the cheapest hours costs money even when the average is good."}],"notes":{"metrics":"Only hours unpublished at issue time are scored. Naive = same hour on the most recent known day.","intervals":"The likely range is measured from how far the forecasts have actually missed at the same hour of day over the last 90 days; the first 30 days after a series starts have no range.","spike":"Flag when forecast exceeds the trailing 90-day 90th percentile of realised prices. The section stays down while pre